Q: draw image on canvas A: <p>If you don't like to use <code>Promise</code> or <code>onload</code> you can also use <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/EventTarget/addEventListener" rel="nofollow noreferrer">EventListener</a>:</p>
<pre><code>function draw() {
  var ctx = document.getElementById('canvas').getContext('2d');
  var img = new Image();
  img.addEventListener('load', function(){
    ctx.drawImage(img, 0, 0);
  });
  img.src = '/files/4531/backdrop.png';
}
</code></pre>

Q: Codename one label text wraping A: <p>Codename One has a <code>SpanLabel</code> class:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>SpanLabel label = new SpanLabel(
        &quot;A long string that should wrap across multiple lines&quot;
);
label.setTextUIID(&quot;Label&quot;);
</code></pre>
<p>Original answer below:</p>
<p>The label is always a single line label the solution based on text area should work just fine if it doesn't I suggest making sure that you added everything on the EDT (you can activate EDT violation detection in the simulator menu) and if you added the component after the form is shown you should use revalidate(). There is no need to define the row count.</p>
<p>Just for completeness here is how this would work:</p>
<pre><code>TextArea lbl = new TextArea(&quot;My very long string that should break lines because its really really really really long and just won't end&quot;);
lbl.setEditable(false);
lbl.setFocusable(false);
lbl.setUIID(&quot;Label&quot;);
</code></pre>

Q: Textscreen in Codename One, how to read text file? A: <p>In a modern Maven project, place the file under <code>common/src/main/resources</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>try (InputStream in = CN.getResourceAsStream(&quot;/filename.txt&quot;)) {
    String text = Util.readToString(in, &quot;UTF-8&quot;);
}
</code></pre>
<p>The Designer &quot;data section&quot; mentioned in the original answer below is only relevant to legacy projects:</p>
<p>In the Codename One Designer go to the data section and add a file.</p>
<p>You can just add the text there and fetch it using <code>myResFile.getData(&quot;name&quot;);</code>.</p>
<p>You can also store the file within the src directory and get it using <code>Display.getInstance().getResourceAsStream(&quot;/filename.txt&quot;);</code></p>
